  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThe Northern Plains are mostly dominated by zonal flow, with a \nsmall shortwave embedded over Montana currently. A large upper \nlevel low system sits near Vancouver. At the surface the \nprevious cold front has now made it all the way through North \nDakota, so temperatures across western and central North Dakota\nare only in the mid 60s to lower 70s. A surface low pressure \nsits in east central South Dakota, with a pressure gradient \naffecting the eastern half of North Dakota, where winds are \ngusting around 20 mph. That upper level shortwave has created a\nnarrow band of rain showers laid across eastern Montana, on an \neastern track. The NBM has very little PoPs for these showers in\nNorth Dakota, so we had to manually add those in. These showers\nshould track across northern North Dakota and slowly dissipate\nthis evening.\n\nAs this zonal flow continues into the weekend, the below normal\ncooler temperatures will too. Tonight expect lows in the 40s\nagain, then highs Saturday in the low to mid 60s. This trend\nwill continue for Sunday as well. Saturday will be windy across\nthe eastern half of the state as the surface low slides into \nwestern Minnesota, producing a strong pressure gradient across \nNorth Dakota. Northwest winds 15 to 30 mph are forecast. \n\nHeading into Sunday and Monday that upper level low will slide \ninto Montana, creating strong southwest flow over the Northern \nRockies. This will create a surface low pressure that moves into\nthe Dakotas, producing widespread rain Sunday night through \nMonday. With this surface pressure, another strong pressure \ngradient will occur. Sunday winds will be the strongest across \nthe southwest where southeast winds 25 to 40 mph are forecast. \nMonday will be slightly windier with a frontal passage. Current \nNBM forecast winds across the west are 30 to 50mph, lower east.\nWe will keep an eye on this for a possible Wind Advisory or \nWarning. The backside of this system will allow slightly warmer \n850mb temperatures to come back north, warming our temperatures \nback into the mid 70s by the end of next week along with low \nchances for rain.",
